// case: generic-model-type-parameter
// what: the shape that used to be refused is real now. `model Box[T] { v: T }`
//      is one declaration and many structs: the builder fixes T from the value
//      written for the field that uses it and emits one C struct per
//      instantiation, so `Box { v: 1 }` is `Box_int` with an `orbit_int` and
//      `Box { v: "hi" }` is `Box_string` with an `orbit_string`. Nothing
//      substitutes at the emitter any more, which is the whole reason the
//      parser used to say no.
//
//      What is left is the spelling that cannot reach a type argument. There
//      is no grammar for one at an expression site -- `Box<int>` lexes as a
//      comparison, and adding that grammar is a far bigger change than the
//      feature -- so the positional constructor has an argument and nothing to
//      call it an int. The emitter would resolve the call against a `Box` that
//      is never emitted, so this is refused, and the diagnostic says what to
//      write instead.
//
//      The other three refusals this feature needs are n50 (a value that
//      disagrees with what the literal already decided), n51 (a generic model
//      named as a declared type, where there is no literal to infer from) and
//      n52 (a type parameter no field's declared type mentions, so there is no
//      value to take the argument from).
// expect-error: Model 'Box' takes a type parameter, so it is constructed with a literal.
model Box[T] {
    v: T
}

fn main() -> int {
    val b = Box(1)
    print(b.v)
    return 0
}